# KORVANE COLD CHAIN Sp. z o.o. — Employee Handbook, Version 3

Ref. HR-HB-03 · valid from 1 September 2021 · prepared by Human Resources & Administration (Maja Kolasińska, HR Manager) · approved by the Management Board on 7 August 2021 · Employee Council consulted 12 July 2021

**NOTE: this version has been replaced by Version 4 of 1 February 2024 and is kept for the archive only. Figures below are NOT current.**

## Chapter 1 — About this booklet

This booklet sets out, in plain words and as briefly as the lawyers would allow, the main rules of employment at Korvane. It applies to everybody working under a Polish employment contract at the Gdynia depot GDY-1 and at the Hamburg-Waltershof (HAM-2), Rotterdam-Maasvlakte (RTM-3) and Gothenburg-Arendal (GOT-4) depots, subject to local law. There is no Kłaipėda yard yet; when there is one we will add a page. Questions go to the HR office on the second floor, room 2.14, or to hr@korvane.example.

The booklet replaces Version 2 of 2016. It was written, as many of you have noticed, during a summer in which half the office was on holiday and the other half was working out how to use Teams, so please be kind and send corrections.

## Chapter 2 — Starting work

2.1 Every new employee serves a probation period of 2 months. During probation the contract can be ended by either side with 3 working days' notice. The manager meets the new colleague once, at the end of the first month.

2.2 Office hours are 08:00 to 16:00, Monday to Friday, with a 30-minute unpaid lunch break; a tolerance of 15 minutes at the start of the day is allowed but is not meant to be used every day. There is no flexible start in Version 3. Time is recorded on the paper sheet that hangs by the kitchen, and the sheet is collected by the HR office on the last working day of each month.

2.3 Depot staff work in three shifts: 07:00 to 15:00, 15:00 to 23:00 and 23:00 to 07:00. The roster is displayed 7 days in advance. Staff who are late more than 3 times in a month will be asked to explain.

2.4 Overtime must be ordered in advance by the shift supervisor. It is paid at 150% of the hourly rate for the first 2 hours of a day and at 175% for further hours and for work on Sundays and public holidays. Night work attracts a night supplement of 20% of the hourly rate. Instead of payment an employee may take time off at 1.25 hours for each hour of overtime. The Company expects overtime not to exceed 150 hours in a year.

2.5 Salaries are paid on the 12th of the month following the month worked.

## Chapter 3 — Holiday

3.1 Annual leave for full-time employees depends on years of service: under 3 years, 20 working days; from 3 to 5 years, 23 working days; from 6 to 9 years, 26 working days; 10 years and more, 28 working days.

3.2 Holiday requests are made on the paper form L-1, in triplicate (one copy for the employee, one for the manager, one for HR), at least 5 working days before any absence of more than 3 days. The manager should answer within a week.

3.3 Up to 5 days may be carried into the next year and must be used by 31 March. The Company will not allow more than 10 consecutive working days of holiday in the period from 1 to 23 December in depot departments.

3.4 Special leave: 1 day for the employee's own wedding; 2 days on the death of a spouse, child or parent; 1 day on the death of any other close relative. There is no special leave for moving house.

## Chapter 4 — Illness

4.1 If you cannot come to work, ring your shift supervisor or manager within 2 hours of the time you should have started. If you do not ring, the day may be treated as unexcused.

4.2 A medical certificate must be delivered within 5 working days. Employees may stay at home for 1 day without a certificate, but not more than 2 times a year.

4.3 Sick pay: for days 1 to 3 of an absence the Company pays 80% of base pay; for days 4 to 33, 85% of base pay; from day 34 the statutory benefit alone is paid.

## Chapter 5 — Working from home

5.1 Working from home is exceptional and needs the written consent of the Head of Department. It is allowed for at most 1 day a week. During the special rules of 2020–2021 (see Annex 2) up to 5 days a week were allowed; Annex 2 expires on 31 December 2021.

5.2 The Company does not pay a home-office allowance. Equipment is lent for the duration of the arrangement and returned on request.

## Chapter 6 — Business trips and expenses

6.1 Expenses are claimed on form E-4 within 60 days of the end of the trip. Reimbursement is paid on the 12th of the month following the claim.

6.2 Reimbursement limits per person:

| Country | Meals per day | Hotel per night | Private car per km |
|---|---|---|---|
| Poland | PLN 95 | PLN 340 | PLN 1.15 |
| Germany | EUR 30 | EUR 105 | EUR 0.29 |
| Netherlands | EUR 33 | EUR 120 | EUR 0.31 |
| Sweden | EUR 36 | EUR 128 | EUR 0.28 |

6.3 A receipt is needed for every item above PLN 50 (about EUR 12). One glass of wine or beer per head at a client dinner is acceptable up to EUR 15 per head; spirits are not reimbursed.

6.4 Trips estimated above EUR 400 need the approval of the Head of Department; above EUR 2,000 the CFO signs. Entertainment of customers is limited to EUR 55 per head.

## Chapter 7 — Behaviour at work

7.1 Employees are expected to behave decently. Alcohol is forbidden on duty; a breath-alcohol reading above 0.20 per mille is treated as gross misconduct. Falsifying a temperature record, theft and violence are also gross misconduct. (The codes GM-1 to GM-6 were introduced only in Version 4.)

7.2 Gifts from customers worth more than EUR 50 must be reported to the HR office.

7.3 Smoking is allowed only in the smoking shelter behind the depot gatehouse.

7.4 Employees must not talk about customers' cargo or temperature records outside the Company. KC-Tag 3 logs are the property of the customer.

## Chapter 8 — If something goes wrong (discipline)

8.1 Discipline has four steps: (1) a verbal warning, which is noted on the personnel file for 6 months; (2) a written warning, 12 months; (3) a final written warning, 12 months; (4) dismissal.

8.2 A sanction may be imposed only within 14 days after the manager became aware of the matter. The employee is given at least 2 working days' notice of the meeting and may bring a colleague. The decision is given in writing within 7 working days.

8.3 An appeal can be made within 5 calendar days to the Managing Director, who decides within 14 days.

## Chapter 9 — Leaving the Company

9.1 Notice from the Company: 2 weeks if the employee has worked for less than 6 months; 1 month if 6 months to 3 years; 2 months if more than 3 years. Notice from the employee is 1 month, except for heads of department, who give 2 months.

9.2 Company property (keys, badge, protective clothing, laptop) is to be returned within 7 days of the last day. Final pay is due on the 12th of the following month. Unused holiday is paid out at 1/21 of the monthly salary per day.

## Chapter 10 — Training and benefits

10.1 Each employee has PLN 1,500 per year for training of their choice, with the manager's approval.

10.2 The Company pays PLN 70 per month towards the health plan of every employee.

10.3 Employees who have worked for 5 years receive a thank-you gift voucher of PLN 800.

## Annex 2 (extract) — Remote work during the pandemic

Valid until 31 December 2021. Office employees may work from home up to 5 days a week. The Company lends one monitor per employee. Employees must be available on Teams between 09:00 and 15:00.

*Printed by HR office, Gdynia, August 2021. If you spot a mistake, tell Maja.*
